s a Democratic-friendly climate. The result is a coin-flip race with a slight Lombardo tilt.", "watch": "With polling this thin and partisan-skewed, the late advertising blitz and fresh independent surveys could move the race in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Joe Lombardo (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nevada's governor's race is one of the closest in the country, with incumbent Republican Joe Lombardo seeking a second term against Democratic Attorney General Aaron Ford. All six major race raters call it a tossup, and betting markets put Ford's chances between 46 and 48 percent. Polling averages give Lombardo a slim edge of about one point, though the most recent independent survey, from Emerson College in early September, showed Ford up two.\n\nThe polling record is thin and skewed toward partisan sponsors, with Republican-affiliated surveys from the summer showing Lombardo ahead by wide margins and Democratic-affiliated polls showing a near tie. Lombardo benefits from incumbency in a state that has narrowly favored Republicans in recent cycles, while Ford counts on a favorable national climate and a late surge in Democratic advertising. An independent, Danielle Ford, also appears on the ballot.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4762, "margin": -0.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.447, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4885, "margin": -0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4837, "margin": -0.4}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4293, "margin": -0.7}]}